#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221157 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "11b5421d-e98b-5e37-9bdd-bec13f010118" :question/math-academy-id "e-21503" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathcal{L}\\{\\cos(\\omega t)\\}  = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}$ for $s>0,$  find the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle g(t)= \\int_{0}^{t} \\cos\\big(\\pi\\tau\\big) \\, \\textrm{d}\\tau, \\quad s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau) \\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t) = \\cos(\\pi t).$ Thus, for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s > 0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221158 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "58ed1920-cd4f-5993-b3bf-1fa4e43492af" :question/math-academy-id "e-21527" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s(s-1)}, \\qquad s > 1\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\dfrac{1}{s-a},\\ s>a.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s-1}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, \\text{d}\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={e}^{τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221163 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51dfecdb-ad66-5e07-8873-ca8e116b27bd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Formula for Laplace Transforms of Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "602b037e-28bf-5afd-a466-652668bf08c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-21551" :question/problem "Prove that if $\\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\} = F(s)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{\\int_{0}^{t} f(\\tau)\\, d\\tau \\right\\} = \\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A(t)$ be the anti-derivative of $f(t)$ such that $A'(t) = f(t)$ and $A(0) ={ 0}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,dτ & =A(τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =A(t)-A(0) \\\\  & =A(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} & =L\\{A(t)\\}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's use integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=A(t) & \\,⇒\\, & du={A}^{′}(t)\\,dt \\\\ dv={e}^{-st}\\,dt & ⇒ & v=-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}A(t)\\,dt & =A(t)(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st}){A}^{′}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A(0)=0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ we have $-\\dfrac{1}{s}A(t)e^{-st}\\bigg|_0^{\\infty} = {0}$ and\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-st}A(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n= \\dfrac{1}{s}\\int_0^{\\infty}{ e^{-st} A'(t)} \\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFinally, using that $A'(t) = f(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{A}^{′}(t)\\,dt & =\\frac{1}{s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}\\cdot F(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ddb6627-9aab-5952-b2ce-159bb3d012c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300184} {:answer/id #uuid "2fadd4e7-e733-5dd7-9e6e-56bb35b97cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300193} {:answer/id #uuid "4befee37-8f81-5b4e-a2a7-cfb1aae0d977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300357} {:answer/id #uuid "7acb2c8f-f244-5570-a50e-e921d09fa87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300570} {:answer/id #uuid "87393951-93a6-5680-8792-91d8b0b433e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7acb2c8f-f244-5570-a50e-e921d09fa87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300570} :answer-field/id #uuid "44f06649-29ac-5ed0-8693-7d88b83ad795" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "323eedee-f02a-50b8-b6e9-9655eac05ba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-327935" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2})},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2})}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{\\pi }{{s}^{2}+{\\pi }^{2}}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\pi }^{2}}\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65f194c1-4a31-5dc6-9dd1-b74fb4074cce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441970} {:answer/id #uuid "8616a0a9-5e4c-5a5b-b120-d0f5a87babe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s(s-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441975} {:answer/id #uuid "ca93bd69-e78e-5e89-9ce8-c601d0049523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441987} {:answer/id #uuid "e50db842-81cd-53f3-991f-23b6b0d1af22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441996} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e331c7-00aa-5a0a-a9ad-b24650546570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{s-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8616a0a9-5e4c-5a5b-b120-d0f5a87babe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s(s-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441975} :answer-field/id #uuid "61080eee-c12c-5456-b01c-6b7bdcb3d31a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "954d3e87-b1ac-516e-bb73-13e2611b178e" :question/math-academy-id "q-330821" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>3.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{3t}.$ Thus, for $s>3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>3,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31bff94b-20f3-5f6b-a517-e5ef54908756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441950} {:answer/id #uuid "4b585c91-a64d-5156-9b47-3ab3be96c10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441963} {:answer/id #uuid "7393820c-ee1c-5fad-afb6-cfabaf3f9ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441973} {:answer/id #uuid "a12e3278-78eb-5071-8f80-02016ffa5453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441978} {:answer/id #uuid "d12a242e-7964-5cb0-85a3-8ccfe0400402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b585c91-a64d-5156-9b47-3ab3be96c10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441963} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3e1d20-c247-5e43-896a-e0df80d1c646" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1980651-68dd-5422-8d9a-998a7b723dd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-330822" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={t}^{2}.$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{2}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442018 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07d7257d-8558-5174-b584-bebeccdc3e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{4s-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441939} {:answer/id #uuid "20e282f4-88ec-52d6-8ea7-9a6576df4b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4s-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441943} {:answer/id #uuid "344220eb-e137-5ef4-9290-8367a1f2b18c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441952} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3d659d-d6ec-5e9b-87ec-2f606fd5411d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s(4s-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441959} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fcf8bd-187b-515e-98be-9d23bc084284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4s-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c3d659d-d6ec-5e9b-87ec-2f606fd5411d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s(4s-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441959} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c3a0a01-7462-57a7-ba8f-e0b8d49c956f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "056683d5-9c10-553b-93b1-01f7db0cfa24" :question/math-academy-id "q-330828" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{(3/4)τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>\\frac{3}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={e}^{(3/4)t}.$ Thus, for $s>\\frac{3}{4},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{(3/4)t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s-\\frac{3}{4})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>\\frac{3}{4},$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{(3/4)τ}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s(s-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{s(4s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06802c47-a352-526d-aa42-6ea70b69cd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302499} {:answer/id #uuid "31006540-4f0e-543e-bc68-5adb6ecfd2d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302764} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6ba575-0c9b-5053-9fbd-17ede9965369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{{s}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302826} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc36011-bc52-5787-9951-7552a3914c43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303431} {:answer/id #uuid "be43cfb0-d748-5bad-a011-749c230baa15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be43cfb0-d748-5bad-a011-749c230baa15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303621} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d4daa5-138e-5de2-a176-e7cd9c9e605f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "104a4fc1-fc3a-583e-ac36-a24bfc2670cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-330831" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{3}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)={t}^{3}.$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{t}^{3}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{3!}{{s}^{3+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{3}\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012cec05-92d1-5798-a767-621527d58c68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302456} {:answer/id #uuid "3857e50f-adec-5e95-9bb0-3b493942d1f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302804} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad49800-edde-5768-ace9-4dcfe368023f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302927} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb496d2-2774-5dab-abdc-4cbe2096ec4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303343} {:answer/id #uuid "c332c53f-6fbe-5b2e-9025-eb87ebb84156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ad49800-edde-5768-ace9-4dcfe368023f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302927} :answer-field/id #uuid "4618ac30-5dc6-5b42-a655-bca47677562b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "510ae8c4-1f31-5a18-9a74-826f9d3a4047" :question/math-academy-id "q-330835" :question/problem "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}$ for $s>0$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$ Thus, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =L\\{f(t)\\} \\\\  & =L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the integral identity above, we have that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{g(t)\\} & =L\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{F(s)}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a3c959-75ca-5441-bcf3-23d4f787222c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302479} {:answer/id #uuid "1bef64cc-0d2c-5f98-a507-02d1052185e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302638} {:answer/id #uuid "71add469-2d90-55dc-be03-2e3a39950b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303145} {:answer/id #uuid "8487079f-256c-548b-9b50-71e2a3f7c818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303282} {:answer/id #uuid "b14358a0-e002-52d4-aaa1-35404f1569bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71add469-2d90-55dc-be03-2e3a39950b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303145} :answer-field/id #uuid "36d309ae-5289-56fe-8e78-f5864b8353f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "409ed1af-a518-5652-a26f-cb341eaa1c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-330838" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+4)},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s({s}^{2}+4)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2τ)\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a07a90-d683-57da-8fc8-5a970843054f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441940} {:answer/id #uuid "373e9c97-d5d1-54a7-a3e3-56e016bbff64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441953} {:answer/id #uuid "60ee170d-1656-50f2-9512-e65dc86bd1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441968} {:answer/id #uuid "b052a9f1-3e7a-53f2-a98d-0235aab679a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{4}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441979} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdb9b67-f22e-5c9d-b541-183acbc514e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10a07a90-d683-57da-8fc8-5a970843054f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441940} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c6a3d3-7631-583a-b8b1-21c64913429c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ffb65743-685e-5524-b4b3-34401a7e30c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-330840" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{4}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{2!}{{s}^{2+1}}\\} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{2}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f80b316-6731-5f5a-9dec-739fd3be6c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{720}{{t}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302559} {:answer/id #uuid "25d0f0bf-2b54-519f-94e7-adf5da66e6c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302700} {:answer/id #uuid "42d5d705-b17e-599c-8fd8-42db38dbf6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{8}}{56}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302880} {:answer/id #uuid "902b0ead-51de-5d7b-bd8f-d5add3c46114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303356} {:answer/id #uuid "b6cdff7d-b0fc-5867-b70b-88bd55c14f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6cdff7d-b0fc-5867-b70b-88bd55c14f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303589} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e69e93c-a037-567a-be3b-ba40f212fcb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37553079-b450-5c8e-9348-e4ed61dd6d77" :question/math-academy-id "q-330845" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{8}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{8}}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{720}{{s}^{7}}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{n}\\}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{720}{{s}^{7}}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{6!}{{s}^{6+1}}\\} \\\\  & ={t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{τ}^{6}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a65eeb21-bcd5-542d-8277-04dab7fa9b53" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Inverse Laplace Transform Using the Integral Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27216b69-d2aa-588b-b25c-49b94f88c580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{t}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441946} {:answer/id #uuid "27a1e96a-2d02-5798-a435-19834cb6e962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{4t}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441947} {:answer/id #uuid "3a067135-e5b7-55d1-925b-a9eb022c03a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(1-{e}^{4t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441956} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf7be69-473e-55c0-88f8-9515389c2337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441982} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa42c98-56c6-5b90-8cdd-cf61b5f56f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27a1e96a-2d02-5798-a435-19834cb6e962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{4t}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441947} :answer-field/id #uuid "998aa03e-483d-5aca-ad28-ce026cba4873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9ec2e0a-7b21-59e9-965b-b9f812cb11e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-330846" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-4)},\\,s>4\n$$\n\nfind the inverse Laplace transform of $G(s),$ given that\n$L\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a},\\text{ }s>a.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can write $G(s)$ as \n\n$$\nG(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s-4)}=\\frac{F(s)}{s},\n$$\n\nwhere $F(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4}.$\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\nL\\{{e}^{at}\\}=\\frac{1}{s-a}.\n$$\n\nThus, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we know that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s)$, then\n\n$$\nL\\{{\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ\\}=\\frac{F(s)}{s}.\n$$\n\nUsing the property above in reverse, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}\\{G(s)\\} & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{F(s)}{s}\\} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(τ)\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{4τ}\\,\\text{d}τ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4τ}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{4t}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "51dfecdb-ad66-5e07-8873-ca8e116b27bd" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Formula for Laplace Transforms of Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43529d94-eb1a-54f8-a97a-d90e5c2dedc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441960} {:answer/id #uuid "438c51eb-af54-5958-b35d-17a8fa5ebd0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441961} {:answer/id #uuid "54554302-b24e-52f9-93a0-106165f07b79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441966} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe76cf3-19fc-53fb-a0cd-ade7e04d7261" :answer/typ